course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 30
title: Granny's rules, superstitions and good manners
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At Sashko's granny's door you hear "bud laska, prokhodte" — please come through — then "sidai", sit down, and soon a shout down the hall: "isty!", come and eat!
explain: Granny has rules, and some are old superstitions: don't put your hat or keys on the table ("ne kladit na stIl"), don't whistle indoors ("ne svystY v khati"), don't hand things across the doorway ("ne cherez porih").
explain: Nobody can prove a whistle blows money away or a key on the table brings money troubles — they are beliefs, not facts — but in her house you simply follow them, the way you'd take your shoes off.
explain: Before a long trip the whole family sits down in silence for a moment: "prysIdmo na dorIzhku", let's sit before the road, an old custom for luck.
explain: And to win a granny's heart, use the polite "vy": "vy hArno vyhlyadaiete" (you look lovely) and "yak vashe zdorov'ya?" (how is your health?) — and be ready for a long answer.
fact: "vy hArno vyhlyadaiete" means "you look lovely", a compliment to a granny at a festival, using the polite "vy", not "ty".
fact: "ne mozhna!" means "you mustn't! not allowed!", a firm no from an adult, and you stop at once.
fact: "sidai" means "sit down" when said to a child; to adults you say "sidaite".
fact: "isty!" means "come and eat! dinner's ready!", and the fuller form is "idit isty!", come and eat.
fact: "ne kladit na stIl" means "don't put it on the table"; grannies say it about hats, bags and keys, from an old belief that they bring money troubles.
fact: "ne svystY v khati" means "don't whistle in the house"; the superstition says whistling indoors "whistles the money away".
fact: "ne cherez porih" means "not across the threshold": many families still avoid handing things or shaking hands across a doorway, so you step inside first.
fact: "prysIdmo na dorIzhku" means "let us sit down before the journey": the family sits quietly for a moment before leaving on a trip, an old custom for luck.
fact: Superstitions like these are beliefs, not proven facts, but a polite guest follows the house rules.
fact: "bud laska, prokhodte" means "please come through", said by the host showing a guest in, and the answer is "DYAkuyu".
fact: "yak vashe zdorov'ya?" means "how is your health?", a caring polite question that grandparents appreciate.
fact: "pane" means "sir" and "pani" means "madam" when addressing someone; they are very respectful, and a child may say "pane" to the friend's grandad.
exercise: At a festival you want to compliment Sashko's granny. What do you say? | vy hArno vyhlyadaiete | ty hArno vyhlyadaiesh | ty shcho, durnyi? | sam takYi!
exercise: Why does "vy hArno vyhlyadaiete" use "vy"? | It is the polite form for an elder | It is said to many people only | It is the word for "you look" | It is the informal form for friends
exercise: You reach for the stove and Sashko's mum says "ne mozhna!". What do you do? | Stop at once | Carry on carefully | Ask "chomu?" and keep going | Laugh and say "krutO!"
exercise: Sashko's granny points at a chair: "sidai". What does she want? | You to sit down | You to stand up straight | You to go home now | You to wash your hands
exercise: How would you invite Sashko's dad to sit down politely? | sidaite | sidai | sidai, pani | sidaite, durnyk
exercise: Sashko's mum calls "isty!" down the hall. What does it mean? | Come and eat, dinner's ready | Come and wash your hands now | Go to sleep | Tidy your room
exercise: You put your woolly hat on the table and granny says "ne kladit na stIl". What should you do? | Move it off the table | Put it on your head at the table | Leave it, it's fine | Put your keys next to it
exercise: What is the old belief behind "ne kladit na stIl" for keys and bags? | They bring money troubles | They scratch the table and spoil it | They make the food cold | They bring rain
exercise: You start whistling in the kitchen and granny says "ne svystY v khati". What is the old belief? | Whistling indoors whistles the money away | Whistling wakes the baby | Whistling calls the rain | Whistling scares the cat
exercise: Sashko's mum is at the door and you hold out a present across the doorway. She says "ne cherez porih". What do you do? | Step inside first, then hand it over | Throw it to her | Give it to Sashko instead | Leave it on the step
exercise: Before a long car trip Sashko's dad says "prysIdmo na dorIzhku". What happens? | Everyone sits quietly for a moment | Everyone packs the car | Everyone sings a song for good luck | Everyone eats a quick snack
exercise: At the door Sashko's granny says "bud laska, prokhodte". What does she mean? | Please come through | Please go away for now | Please take a seat here | Please wait outside the door
exercise: What does "yak vashe zdorov'ya?" mean? | How is your health? | How old are you now? | How was the meal today? | How is your house and garden?
exercise: What happens when you ask a Ukrainian granny "yak vashe zdorov'ya?"? | She is pleased and answers at length | She is offended | She says "ne mozhna!" | She gives you homework to do for tomorrow
exercise: How do you politely call to Sashko's grandad? | pane | pani | brat | durnyk
exercise: How do you politely address a woman shopkeeper? | pani | pane | TItka | podRUha
test: my mate says whistling indoors really does lose you money. right? | No, it's a superstition, not a fact | Yes, it's proven by banks and scientists | Yes, but only in Ukraine | No, it loses you friends
test: Your mate says "a superstition is silly, just ignore granny's rule". What is the polite thing? | Follow the house rule anyway | Argue with granny until she agrees | Whistle louder to prove her wrong | Leave the house and never come back
test: What would happen if you said "ty hArno vyhlyadaiesh" to Sashko's granny? | It'd sound too casual: use "vy" with elders | She'd find it extra polite | She'd think you said goodbye | She'd think you were asking her age
test: The family stands up, bags ready, then everyone suddenly sits down in silence. Why? | prysIdmo na dorIzhku: sitting before a journey for luck | Someone forgot the keys | They're waiting for a taxi only | They're too tired to go
test: Sashko's granny says "isty!" and you shout "zaraz!" and keep playing for twenty minutes. How will she feel? | Annoyed: dinner is getting cold | Pleased you're busy with your game | She won't mind at all | She'll bring the food to you
test: In a cartoon, a boy answers "ne mozhna!" by doing it anyway. What does "ne mozhna!" really ask? | That you stop at once | That you try once more | That you ask nicely first | That you do it quickly
test: You call Sashko's mum "pane". What slip did you make? | pane is for a man; she is "pani" | pane is for children and young people | pane means "mum" | No slip, pane is for women
test: Sashko's granny shakes her head when you try to shake hands across the doorway. Why? | Many families avoid things across the threshold | She doesn't like handshakes at all | Her hands are wet | She is angry with you
test: Your bag goes on the dinner table and granny frowns. Best move? | Put it on the floor or a chair | Put your keys on it too | Explain it's a myth and leave it there | Ask for more soup
test: Why do Ukrainian grannies love "yak vashe zdorov'ya?" | It shows you care about them | It means "you look old" in a nice way | It asks for sweets | It is a joke question
test: Granny says "sidai" to Sashko and "sidaite" to his dad. Why the difference? | sidai to a child, sidaite politely to an adult | sidai means stand, sidaite means sit | sidaite is for girls | No difference, it's random
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the injunction "ne svystY v khati"? | It is a household superstition holding that indoor whistling dispels wealth, not an established fact | It is a scientifically established principle of acoustics | It is a regulation of the Ukrainian government | It is a reprimand for excessive noise at night only
exam: Identify the custom denoted by the expression "prysIdmo na dorIzhku". | A brief, silent sitting of the household prior to departure on a journey | The serving of a meal prior to departure | The packing of provisions for a journey | The farewell embrace at the railway station
exam: It is given that a guest wishes to compliment an elderly lady. Select the formulation appropriate to her seniority. | vy hArno vyhlyadaiete | ty hArno vyhlyadaiesh | sam takYi! | pryVIT!
exam: Ascertain the action required of a child upon hearing an adult pronounce "ne mozhna!". | Immediate cessation of the action concerned | Continuation of the action with greater care | Repetition of the action to seek approval | Enquiry as to the time of day
